course: dictionary
lesson: 748
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The dictionary lists them as separate senses, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «In a relay you pass the baton into the next runner's hand» shows a baton as a stick in a race, because of the words relay and runner.
explain: The same word can mean something very different in another sentence. «The conductor keeps dozens of players together» shows a baton as the thin stick that leads an orchestra or choir, because a conductor and players belong to music. Ask yourself: where is this happening, and who is doing it?
explain: Some phrases use a word in a new way. «Pass the baton» can mean to hand over a job to someone else, so it is not really about a stick at all. Try the simple meaning in the sentence first. If it does not fit, look at the clues around it.
fact: Some words have more than one meaning, and each meaning is called a sense.
fact: A baton is a short stick passed from one runner to the next in a relay race.
fact: A baton can also be a thin stick a conductor waves to lead an orchestra or choir.
fact: A baton can be a stick that some police officers carry, or a stick twirled by a majorette or drum major in a parade.
fact: The phrase «pass the baton» means to hand over a job to someone else.
exercise: «In a relay you pass the baton into the next runner's hand.» What is a baton in this sentence? | A thin stick for leading a choir | *A short stick passed in a race | A long rope for climbing | A stick for hitting a ball
exercise: Which clue in «The conductor keeps dozens of players together» tells you the baton is a music stick? | *conductor | together | dozens | keeps
exercise: Dad says, «It's your turn to pass the baton on the project.» What does pass the baton mean? | To drop the project on the floor | *To hand over the job to someone else | To run very fast with a stick | To wave a stick at the band
test: «The police officer carried a baton on his belt.» What is a baton in this sentence? | A thin stick a conductor waves | A short stick passed in a relay race | A stick twirled in a parade | *A stick some police officers carry
test: «The majorette twirled her baton at the front of the parade.» What is a baton here? | *A stick twirled by a majorette in a parade | A job handed to someone else | A thin stick for leading a choir | A stick passed between runners
exam: «She dropped the baton and the team lost the race.» Which meaning fits? | *A short stick passed from runner to runner | A thin stick a conductor waves | To hand over a job to someone else | A stick some police officers carry
